      "notes": "All claims are adequately supported by their cited evidence. Tier assignments are correct: stated claims quote the manifesto text directly; projected claims are appropriately flagged. The measurable claim using E9 (Commons Library) is borderline \u2014 E9 is itself a summary/projection ('would therefore largely codify') rather than a raw fact \u2014 but the framing in the claim mirrors the quote closely enough not to constitute overstating. The 'mixed' direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides (liberty restriction on trans people vs. liberty gain for biological women), avoiding false_balance. Advocacy sources (sex-matters.org, equity.org.uk, policyexchange.org.uk) are used symmetrically on both sides and are not the sole basis for any verdict direction. The rationale attributes no motive or intent to the Conservative Party and is grounded in policy effect. Magnitude and time_horizon are not n/a given direction is 'mixed' not 'too-uncertain', which is correct. No lever_leakage detected \u2014 the verdict judges effect on O10 liberty interests, not the policy as a lever. One minor note: E19 is backed partly by hks.harvard.edu (academic) and equity.org.uk (advocacy); the claim accurately characterises it as 'legal analyses suggest' which is appropriately hedged."
